  <section id="checking-whether-orca-can-access-an-application">
    <title>Checking whether Orca can access an application</title>
    <p>
      If Orca does not present an application, leave that application running and run <code>orca -l</code> in a terminal. This lists the
      applications known to AT-SPI, the accessibility service Orca uses.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the application is not listed, Orca has no way of knowing it exists. The application is
      not exposing an accessibility tree or accessibility events to Orca, so Orca cannot present
      its content or respond to changes within it.
    </p>
    <p>
      An application being listed does not guarantee that all of its content is accessible, but
      it confirms that AT-SPI knows about the application.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="refreshing-an-application-s-accessibility-information">
    <title>Refreshing an application's accessibility information</title>
    <p>
      AT-SPI caches accessibility information about an application's objects. It clears affected
      cached information in response to accessibility events from the application or its
      toolkit. Normally, Orca should receive current information without manual intervention.
    </p>
    <section id="commands-refreshing-an-application-s-accessibility-information">
      <title>Commands</title>
      <list>
        <item>
          <p>
            <gui>Clear the AT-SPI cache for the current application</gui>: Unbound by
            default.
          </p>
          <p>
            Clears cached AT-SPI information for the current application so Orca can request
            fresh information. If Orca is presenting incorrect or outdated information, assign a
            shortcut in Commands preferences, return to the affected application, use the
            command, and try the interaction again. If this restores correct presentation,
            mention it when reporting the problem: it suggests that AT-SPI's cached
            accessibility information was stale, potentially due to a bug in the application or
            its toolkit.
          </p>
        </item>
      </list>
    </section>
