  <section id="testing-speech-output">
    <title>Testing speech output</title>
    <p>
      If Orca is not speaking, test its speech system independently from a terminal. For Speech
      Dispatcher, run <code>spd-say "This is a speech test."</code>. For Spiel, run <code>spiel "This is a speech test."</code>. You may need to
      install the corresponding command-line tool first.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the command does not produce speech either, the problem is with the speech system, its
      synthesizer, or your audio output, rather than Orca alone. If it does produce speech,
      check Orca's selected speech system, synthesizer, and voice: the command may use different
      defaults. Both tools let you specify a voice and other options. Run <code>spd-say --help</code> or <code>spiel --help</code> for details.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="if-orca-remains-unresponsive">
    <title>If Orca remains unresponsive</title>
    <p>
      If Orca does not respond in other applications either, restart it using your desktop's
      screen reader controls. If you started Orca as a regular application, you can instead run <code>orca --replace</code>. See "<link xref="introduction">Starting and stopping Orca</link>" for the
      distinction between these ways of running Orca.
    </p>
    <p>
      See also:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <link xref="features_settings_backup">Backing up, restoring, and resetting settings</link>: Back up your
          settings before testing with defaults, then restore them afterward.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
  <section id="collecting-debugging-information">
    <title>Collecting debugging information</title>
    <p>
      Start Orca with verbose debugging enabled using <code>orca --debug</code>, or <code>orca --debug-file FILE</code> to choose the log
      destination. If Orca is already running as an application, add <code>--replace</code>, for example <code>orca --replace --debug</code>. If your desktop
      manages Orca as a service, stop it using the desktop's screen reader controls first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Debug logs can become very large. If possible, limit your activity while logging to the
      steps needed to demonstrate the problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      Debug logs contain detailed information about your actions and interactions with
      applications while logging is enabled, including application content. Review logs for
      private information before sharing them.
    </p>
    <section id="commands-debugging-tools">
      <title>Commands</title>
      <list>
        <item>
          <p>
            <gui>Cycle the debug level at run time</gui>: Unbound by
            default.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cycles through all messages, informational messages, warnings, severe errors, and
            off.
          </p>
        </item>
      </list>
    </section>
  </section>
  <section id="getting-help-and-reporting-problems">
    <title>Getting help and reporting problems</title>
    <p>
      The Orca mailing list is where most Orca-related communication takes place. Its
      knowledgeable, helpful user community shares advice and discusses problems and ideas. The
      list archive is searchable, so you can look for earlier discussions of your question.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can file bug reports and feature requests in Orca's GitLab project. For a bug report,
      include your Orca version, the affected application and its version, the steps to
      reproduce the problem, and what you expected compared with what happened. Include the
      results of any troubleshooting steps you tried.
    </p>
    <p>
      See also:
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <link href="https://www.freelists.org/list/orca">Orca mailing list</link>: Subscribe to the
          list or search its archive.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <link href="https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/orca">Orca on GitLab</link>: Report bugs and
          request features.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
